    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nLet relative acceleration be a_rel = a\u2081 - 1. For overtake: s_rel = u_rel t + 1/2 a_rel t\u00b2 \u2265 200. With u_rel = 12 - 10 = 2 m/s: 2t + 1/2 a_rel t\u00b2 \u2265 200. If a\u2081 = 1, a_rel = 0, max distance in finite time or 2t = 200 => t = 100 s! At t = 100 s, if a\u2081 = 1 m/s\u00b2, relative acceleration is 0, relative speed is constant 2 m/s, so it covers 200 m in 100 s! Thus a\u2081 = 1 m/s\u00b2 is sufficient!\n\n2. GIVEN:\nu\u2081 = 12 m/s, u\u2082 = 10 m/s, separation = 200 m, a\u2082 = 1 m/s\u00b2.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\ns_rel = u_rel t + (1/2) a_rel t\u00b2\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Relative initial velocity of car 1 w.r.t car 2: u_rel = 12 - 10 = +2 m/s.\n2. Relative acceleration: a_rel = a\u2081 - a\u2082 = a\u2081 - 1 m/s\u00b2.\n3. Relative displacement needed to overtake: s_rel = 200 m.\n4. If a_rel = 0 (i.e. a\u2081 = 1 m/s\u00b2), the relative velocity remains constant at +2 m/s.\n5. At constant relative velocity of 2 m/s, time to cover 200 m is t = 200 / 2 = 100 s.\n6. Therefore, car 1 WILL overtake car 2 even with a\u2081 = 1 m/s\u00b2! Hence minimum acceleration is 1 m/s\u00b2.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption B: 1 m/s\u00b2\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nIf relative acceleration is zero, relative velocity is constant. Since u\u2081 > u\u2082, overtake will always occur eventually!",
