    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nWork-Energy Theorem: Work done by retarding force W = F \u00d7 s = Initial KE. Since both have the SAME kinetic energy and experience the SAME retarding force F, their stopping distances are identical: s = KE / F.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nKE_truck = KE_car, F_truck = F_car.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nWork = F \u00d7 s = KE => s = KE / F\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. By the Work-Energy Theorem, the work done by the braking force brings the vehicle to rest: F \u00d7 s = KE.\n2. Stopping distance: s = KE / F.\n3. Since both vehicles have identical initial kinetic energies (KE_truck = KE_car) and experience identical braking forces (F_truck = F_car):\n   s_truck = KE / F = s_car.\n4. Therefore, both vehicles stop in the EXACT same distance!\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Both cover the exact same distance\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nCLASSIC NEET TRAP: If KE is the same and F is the same, distance is the same! (If linear MOMENTUM was the same, the heavier vehicle would stop in less distance).",
