    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nWhen a body starts from rest, accelerates at \u03b1, and then decelerates at \u03b2 to rest, total distance is $s = \\frac{v_{\\max}^2}{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{\\alpha} + \\frac{1}{\\beta}\\right)$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\n\u03b1 = 2 m/s\u00b2, \u03b2 = 4 m/s\u00b2, total distance s = 300 m.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\ns = \\frac{v_{\\max}^2}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{\\alpha\\beta}\\right)\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Distance during acceleration: s\u2081 = v_max\u00b2 / (2\u03b1) = v_max\u00b2 / 4.\n2. Distance during deceleration: s\u2082 = v_max\u00b2 / (2\u03b2) = v_max\u00b2 / 8.\n3. Total distance: s = s\u2081 + s\u2082 = v_max\u00b2 (1/4 + 1/8) = v_max\u00b2 (3/8) = 300 m.\n4. v_max\u00b2 = 300 \u00d7 8 / 3 = 100 \u00d7 8 = 800.\n5. v_max = \u221a800 = \u221a(400 \u00d7 2) = 20\u221a2 m/s.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 20\u221a2 m/s\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nQuick NEET formula: $v_{\\max} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 s \\alpha \\beta}{\\alpha + \\beta}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 300 \\times 8}{6}} = \\sqrt{800} = 20\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m/s}$.",
