    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nThe equation of motion $h = ut - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2$ is a quadratic in $t$ whose roots are $t_1$ and $t_2$. The sum of roots is $t_1 + t_2 = \\frac{2u}{g}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nHeight h reached at times t\u2081 (ascent) and t\u2082 (descent).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nt\u2081 + t\u2082 = \\frac{2u}{g} => u = \\frac{g(t\u2081 + t\u2082)}{2}\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Set up the vertical position equation: h = ut - (1/2) g t\u00b2.\n2. Rearrange as standard quadratic in t: (1/2) g t\u00b2 - u t + h = 0 => g t\u00b2 - 2u t + 2h = 0.\n3. The roots of this equation are the two times t\u2081 and t\u2082.\n4. By Vieta's formulas, sum of roots: t\u2081 + t\u2082 = -(-2u) / g = 2u / g.\n5. Solving for u gives: u = g(t\u2081 + t\u2082) / 2.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: g (t\u2081 + t\u2082) / 2\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nUseful NEET relations: (1) $u = \\frac{g(t_1 + t_2)}{2}$, (2) $h = \\frac{1}{2}gt_1 t_2$.",
